WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:08.755
The world has lost 68 percent
of its wildlife populations

00:00:08.755 --> 00:00:10.340
in under 50 years,

00:00:10.382 --> 00:00:12.175
and there are people around the world

00:00:12.175 --> 00:00:15.428
working to protect and grow
the wildlife that is left.

00:00:16.471 --> 00:00:18.139
In Africa, however,

00:00:18.181 --> 00:00:20.100
the approach to conserving this wildlife

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.479
has almost always involved
a separation of people from nature,

00:00:24.521 --> 00:00:28.108
the involvement, but never
leadership, from local people,

00:00:28.149 --> 00:00:32.237
and a problem statement that has often
come from outside our continent.

00:00:32.821 --> 00:00:36.908
Basically years ago, colonial governments
decided that we, as Africans,

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.244
were not fit to take care
of our own wildlife.

00:00:39.286 --> 00:00:43.832
And so people who had lived
alongside wildlife for generations

00:00:43.873 --> 00:00:46.459
were removed from their ancestral lands

00:00:46.459 --> 00:00:48.336
and called new names.

00:00:48.336 --> 00:00:52.090
Poachers, encroachers, squatters.

00:00:53.341 --> 00:00:55.552
The story of conservation, as a result,

00:00:55.594 --> 00:00:59.514
has almost always then involved
only a foreign scientist

00:00:59.556 --> 00:01:00.724
with a clipboard

00:01:00.724 --> 00:01:02.517
or a guy in green with a gun,

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:05.770
there to protect that wildlife
from everyone else.

00:01:05.812 --> 00:01:09.399
The rest of us have never
existed in this story.

00:01:10.108 --> 00:01:14.195
And those who came to save species,
came from the outside.

00:01:14.237 --> 00:01:17.324
And when they came,
they were labeled heroes.

00:01:17.365 --> 00:01:21.202
They had to teach local people
how to live alongside wildlife

00:01:21.244 --> 00:01:23.955
on the fringes of wild lands
that they used to own.

00:01:26.249 --> 00:01:28.585
This has created two distinct problems.

00:01:28.627 --> 00:01:32.047
One, because we don't often
tell our own stories,

00:01:32.047 --> 00:01:35.842
it means that those
who are closest to the wildlife

00:01:35.884 --> 00:01:39.888
are not seen as invested
in conserving that wildlife

00:01:39.929 --> 00:01:42.140
compared to those
who've come from the outside.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.517
And because foreign conservationists

00:01:44.559 --> 00:01:47.020
have sometimes not taken
into consideration

00:01:47.062 --> 00:01:48.730
the needs of local people,

00:01:48.772 --> 00:01:53.318
they are then seen as caring more
for animal life than for human life.

00:01:54.861 --> 00:01:58.239
If we do not change this approach
to conservation in Africa,

00:01:58.281 --> 00:02:00.200
we will lose all of our wildlife

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.078
and with it, a part of our humanity.

00:02:04.079 --> 00:02:10.377
I believe that the time for Africans
to define conservation ourselves has come.

00:02:10.669 --> 00:02:15.590
(Applause)

00:02:15.632 --> 00:02:18.718
And when Africa leads
its own conservation efforts,

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.680
we will not only restore
our wildlife populations

00:02:21.721 --> 00:02:24.307
but our land and our cultures

00:02:24.349 --> 00:02:27.018
and our broken relationship with nature.

00:02:28.019 --> 00:02:30.689
Through my work with Ewaso Lions,

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:33.775
an organization based in northern Kenya

00:02:33.775 --> 00:02:35.694
doing lion conservation,

00:02:35.735 --> 00:02:38.822
I am working with a group of people

00:02:38.822 --> 00:02:43.660
who, together we are co-designing
what that conservation could look like.

00:02:43.702 --> 00:02:46.287
But first, a little about myself.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:52.669
I grew up in a crumbling bungalow
in the heart of Nairobi,

00:02:52.711 --> 00:02:54.295
Kenya's capital city.

00:02:54.671 --> 00:02:59.175
Long before it was called
Nairobi by the Maasai,

00:02:59.217 --> 00:03:02.303
the nomadic pastoralists
where I get my heritage,

00:03:02.345 --> 00:03:04.097
they had called it a different name.

00:03:04.556 --> 00:03:06.558
Nakusontelon.

00:03:06.558 --> 00:03:08.810
"The beginning of all beauty."

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:12.772
As they would graze their cows
and goats on the banks of the river,

00:03:12.814 --> 00:03:16.609
they would watch the evening sun
creep down the acacia trees.

00:03:16.943 --> 00:03:19.446
That was their vision of beauty.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:22.782
Centuries later, I would do the same,

00:03:22.824 --> 00:03:25.952
I would watch the monkeys
in the giant trees,

00:03:25.994 --> 00:03:29.372
and colorful birds would call
to each other in the morning.

00:03:29.372 --> 00:03:31.541
In October, when the nandi flame trees

00:03:31.583 --> 00:03:35.378
would drop the last
of their fiery orangy flowers

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:37.922
that we would use for hopscotch in school,

00:03:37.964 --> 00:03:43.511
there would be thousands and thousands
of jacaranda trees in full bloom

00:03:43.511 --> 00:03:44.679
across the city,

00:03:44.721 --> 00:03:47.515
reminding us that it was
the start of exam season.

00:03:47.515 --> 00:03:48.683
(Laughter)

00:03:48.725 --> 00:03:50.059
"Have you studied?

00:03:50.059 --> 00:03:51.227
Are you ready?"

00:03:51.269 --> 00:03:52.604
they seemed to say.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.648
We were just a part of nature.

00:03:55.648 --> 00:03:57.192
It was just a fact.

00:03:58.151 --> 00:04:00.111
And then the chainsaws came.

00:04:00.612 --> 00:04:03.323
They cut down so much of what I loved,

00:04:03.323 --> 00:04:05.200
they cut down my memories.

00:04:05.241 --> 00:04:07.535
And they have kept coming
not just to my city,

00:04:07.577 --> 00:04:09.078
but to places around the world,

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.624
and not just for trees,
but for everything.

00:04:13.625 --> 00:04:16.503
Let me put some numbers here
so you understand what I mean.

00:04:17.712 --> 00:04:24.511
Lions have lost 92 percent of the area
that they used to roam in Africa.

00:04:25.595 --> 00:04:30.809
Out of a possible 100,000 lions
maybe just a century ago,

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:35.480
there are now only about 20,000
lions left in Africa.

00:04:35.522 --> 00:04:40.610
And in Kenya, there are only
2,500 lions left or thereabouts.

00:04:41.736 --> 00:04:45.031
So what do you do when
you're confronted with such loss?

00:04:46.324 --> 00:04:49.118
The answer for me was to study.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:51.246
And so at the University of Nairobi,

00:04:51.287 --> 00:04:54.249
equipped with new zoological expertise,

00:04:54.249 --> 00:04:57.168
I was informed that I could go out

00:04:57.168 --> 00:05:01.714
and teach local people
how to live alongside wildlife.

00:05:04.884 --> 00:05:06.511
Where did that thinking come from,

00:05:06.511 --> 00:05:09.556
that I could go out
and teach people how to live?

00:05:10.557 --> 00:05:11.933
At the University of Oxford,

00:05:11.933 --> 00:05:13.268
I took my studies further,

00:05:13.309 --> 00:05:16.938
and I really began to unearth
the conservation models

00:05:16.938 --> 00:05:19.107
that had led us to this point.

00:05:19.148 --> 00:05:21.401
And while my studies have provided a frame

00:05:21.442 --> 00:05:24.070
with which to view what was happening,

00:05:24.112 --> 00:05:28.032
it is really on the ground
in my country doing the work

00:05:28.074 --> 00:05:31.202
that I have gained the most
perspective and clarity.

00:05:32.161 --> 00:05:35.290
In the Samburu region of northern Kenya,

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:41.045
there's still little separation between
people and wildlife and livestock.

00:05:41.087 --> 00:05:44.591
Here, you can still hear
the cow bells clanging

00:05:44.591 --> 00:05:50.013
as a little boy brings his goats to water
on the mighty Ewaso Ng’iro river.

00:05:50.054 --> 00:05:53.975
And behind him, nibbling
on the tops of trees, are giraffes.

00:05:54.017 --> 00:05:57.520
And behind that, the rumble of elephants.

00:05:58.271 --> 00:06:02.400
It is here that I have found a group
of people who are pushing back

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:06.112
on that narrative that excludes us
and tells us that we're not fit to lead

00:06:06.154 --> 00:06:10.408
and really building true
community-led conservation.

00:06:11.409 --> 00:06:15.538
There is so much to this approach
that I believe could be important

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:20.209
to form the new Kenyan
and the new African conservation.

00:06:20.877 --> 00:06:23.004
So let me share some of those things.

00:06:23.713 --> 00:06:25.131
First,

00:06:25.173 --> 00:06:31.262
out with parachute conservation
and in with indigenous local leadership.

00:06:31.262 --> 00:06:35.433
(Applause)

00:06:35.475 --> 00:06:38.269
Parachute conservation
might be a new term for some,

00:06:38.269 --> 00:06:41.272
but it's just that old superhero story.

00:06:41.272 --> 00:06:44.025
You jet in, you have all the answers,

00:06:44.025 --> 00:06:46.694
you employ a few people
to effect your solutions,

00:06:46.736 --> 00:06:49.697
and then you leave or you never hand over.

00:06:49.739 --> 00:06:52.116
And the world labels you a hero.

00:06:52.617 --> 00:06:55.745
This sort of conservation
is so detrimental

00:06:55.787 --> 00:07:00.041
because it means that local people
will forever be the helpers

00:07:00.041 --> 00:07:01.709
or the local informants

00:07:01.751 --> 00:07:04.087
and never the leaders
and the decision makers.

00:07:04.128 --> 00:07:06.839
And when that happens, people lose.

00:07:06.881 --> 00:07:09.467
And when people lose, wildlife loses.

00:07:09.842 --> 00:07:11.386
So what's a better way?

00:07:12.345 --> 00:07:14.138
Let me give you an example.

00:07:15.223 --> 00:07:18.810
One of the first people to join
the Ewaso Lions project

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:21.354
was Jeneria Lekilelei,

00:07:21.396 --> 00:07:23.940
a young Samburu warrior at the time,

00:07:23.982 --> 00:07:26.442
and now a junior elder.

00:07:26.484 --> 00:07:29.946
Now this is not some crazy thing
you wouldn't understand.

00:07:30.405 --> 00:07:35.952
A Samburu warrior is just a young man
between the ages of 15 and 30

00:07:35.994 --> 00:07:37.161
or thereabouts,

00:07:37.203 --> 00:07:40.373
and it is his job to take care
of the family's livestock.

00:07:41.582 --> 00:07:45.003
The Samburu and the Maasai
are brother tribes.

00:07:45.378 --> 00:07:49.799
But while I have lost quite a lot
of my Maasai heritage to city life,

00:07:49.799 --> 00:07:53.177
Jeneria still lives
a very traditional Samburu life.

00:07:54.053 --> 00:07:56.014
And so while I was at home,

00:07:56.055 --> 00:07:59.434
watching David Attenborough
and loving lions,

00:07:59.475 --> 00:08:02.311
Jeneria was hating lions.

00:08:02.353 --> 00:08:04.230
He saw them as the killers of his cows,

00:08:04.272 --> 00:08:07.817
and it's understandable
that when a lion comes along

00:08:07.859 --> 00:08:10.445
and takes the family cow
or the family camel,

00:08:10.486 --> 00:08:14.866
there’s going to be anger,
and people will go out and kill lions.

00:08:14.907 --> 00:08:17.118
But Jeneria had an idea.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:22.874
He wanted to involve warriors
like himself in conservation.

00:08:24.167 --> 00:08:27.253
He knew that these warriors
had the exact skills

00:08:27.295 --> 00:08:30.965
to track and kill lions
every time they'd go after livestock.

00:08:30.965 --> 00:08:34.302
He also knew that these warriors

00:08:34.343 --> 00:08:37.430
had never been brought
to the decision-making table.

00:08:37.430 --> 00:08:40.349
And so he brought them, and he said,

00:08:40.391 --> 00:08:42.852
"Instead of us tracking
lions to kill them,

00:08:42.852 --> 00:08:48.232
let us track lions and then tell
every other herder where these lions are,

00:08:48.274 --> 00:08:50.860
so that livestock are safe

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:52.236
and lions are safe.

00:08:52.278 --> 00:08:54.322
And they can share the space.”

00:08:54.322 --> 00:08:58.576
And it is through Jeneria's
Warrior Watch program

00:08:58.576 --> 00:09:02.455
that he has worked with these warriors
as conservation leaders,

00:09:02.497 --> 00:09:05.833
and they have saved lions
hundreds of times in this way.

00:09:06.334 --> 00:09:10.671
And Jeneria, as the director
of community conservation,

00:09:10.671 --> 00:09:14.801
has worked with his community
over these years,

00:09:14.842 --> 00:09:18.304
and the Samburu
lion population has tripled.

00:09:19.597 --> 00:09:26.062
(Applause)

00:09:26.104 --> 00:09:30.233
Next, let us stop merely involving women.

00:09:31.692 --> 00:09:35.488
Women must be as much part
of the solution as men.

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:40.910
And if our imagination for 50 percent
of the world’s human population

00:09:40.952 --> 00:09:43.704
ends at involvement,

00:09:43.704 --> 00:09:45.373
we have already lost.

00:09:45.957 --> 00:09:49.836
So women where I work demanded
to be part of conservation.

00:09:50.294 --> 00:09:51.462
They said --

00:09:51.462 --> 00:09:54.132
(Applause)

00:09:54.465 --> 00:09:58.094
They did this, not just
because they saw the men,

00:09:58.136 --> 00:10:01.097
but because they have
a historical stake in the game.

00:10:01.889 --> 00:10:06.102
In the Samburu creation story,
wildlife belong to women.

00:10:07.019 --> 00:10:10.398
So the story goes
that all the animals in the world

00:10:10.439 --> 00:10:13.234
at the beginning of time
belong to the Samburu,

00:10:13.276 --> 00:10:15.611
and they were all livestock.

00:10:16.362 --> 00:10:19.991
The men were apparently
very good livestock keepers,

00:10:20.032 --> 00:10:23.744
whereas the women
were terrible and irresponsible,

00:10:23.786 --> 00:10:27.999
and they let the livestock
out of the enclosure.

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:30.710
And donkeys became zebras,

00:10:30.751 --> 00:10:33.337
and camels became giraffes.

00:10:33.337 --> 00:10:36.382
And that's how the wildlife
of the world came to be.

00:10:36.924 --> 00:10:40.595
So these women took
this myth and they said,

00:10:40.636 --> 00:10:43.681
"We are turning this patriarchal
myth on its head.

00:10:44.599 --> 00:10:47.727
We are the people who own the wildlife,

00:10:47.727 --> 00:10:50.313
and so you're doing wildlife conservation?

00:10:50.354 --> 00:10:52.273
Then that's our business."

00:10:52.315 --> 00:10:56.652
And one of them who said this was Munteli,

00:10:56.694 --> 00:11:01.073
who is the coordinator
of the Mother of Lions program.

00:11:01.824 --> 00:11:03.743
The Mama Simba program.

00:11:04.076 --> 00:11:07.663
So Munteli said
that as part of their work

00:11:07.705 --> 00:11:11.083
providing lion locations
and forming a home network,

00:11:11.125 --> 00:11:13.502
that all the women, including herself,

00:11:13.544 --> 00:11:15.755
need to be taught to read and write.

00:11:15.796 --> 00:11:18.716
And so they were attending
class once a week.

00:11:18.758 --> 00:11:20.551
And then Munteli came back and said,

00:11:20.593 --> 00:11:23.804
"Actually, you know,
we have far overtaken the men.

00:11:23.804 --> 00:11:27.225
So we have built
an enclosure in our village.

00:11:27.266 --> 00:11:29.769
Bring your teacher and bring
your whiteboard.

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:32.313
We want lessons four times a week."

00:11:32.897 --> 00:11:35.024
And so the women were learning.

00:11:35.066 --> 00:11:36.859
And then Munteli came back and said,

00:11:36.859 --> 00:11:38.986
"You people are not letting me do my work,

00:11:38.986 --> 00:11:42.490
I need to reach women
in so many other villages."

00:11:42.531 --> 00:11:45.910
And so we asked her,
"Munteli, what would you like?"

00:11:46.494 --> 00:11:49.205
And she said, "Teach me how to drive."

00:11:50.081 --> 00:11:56.963
Munteli is now one of the first
traditional Samburu women to drive a car.

00:11:57.296 --> 00:12:03.511
(Applause)

00:12:03.552 --> 00:12:04.720
In her region.

00:12:04.762 --> 00:12:08.474
And she, after just learning
how to read and write

00:12:08.474 --> 00:12:11.102
just a few years ago,

00:12:11.102 --> 00:12:14.814
she texts lion locations
in three different languages.

00:12:15.773 --> 00:12:20.528
She has proved that
the impossible is now possible.

00:12:20.987 --> 00:12:25.283
She has expanded the room for women
to participate in conservation,

00:12:25.324 --> 00:12:27.410
and there is room for all of them.

00:12:29.495 --> 00:12:32.331
Conservation is about people.

00:12:33.332 --> 00:12:35.209
I have learned that the people

00:12:35.251 --> 00:12:38.713
who are keeping lions
roaming in Kenya today

00:12:38.754 --> 00:12:42.800
are warriors and women
and children and elders.

00:12:42.842 --> 00:12:45.511
They are people educated by their culture;

00:12:45.553 --> 00:12:48.848
they are urbanites with a respect
for that culture.

00:12:49.348 --> 00:12:54.562
And as more Africans allow co-existence
to happen in our spaces,

00:12:54.603 --> 00:12:58.232
we will turn back the clock
on wildlife declines

00:12:58.274 --> 00:13:01.152
and really make life better for all of us.

00:13:01.694 --> 00:13:05.323
It is time for conservation to be broad.

00:13:05.781 --> 00:13:10.328
Broad enough not just to include
a species in trouble,

00:13:10.328 --> 00:13:14.123
but our land and our cultures,

00:13:14.123 --> 00:13:18.210
our innovation, our story, us.

00:13:19.045 --> 00:13:22.965
Who we were, who we are
and who we want to be.

00:13:23.382 --> 00:13:24.550
Thank you.

00:13:24.592 --> 00:13:30.723
(Applause)

